<comment datatype='string'>MtrA is the cobamide-binding subunit of &lt;a href=&quot;/META/NEW-IMAGE?type=EC-NUMBER&amp;object=EC-2.1.1.86&quot;&gt;EC 2.1.1.86, tetrahydromethanopterin &lt;em&gt;S&lt;/em&gt;-methyltransferase&lt;/a&gt;. More specifically, it binds a &lt;a href=&quot;/compound?orgid=META&amp;id=CPD-7162&quot;&gt;&lt;i&gt;Co&lt;/i&gt;&amp;alpha;-[&amp;alpha;-(5-hydroxybenzimidazolyl)]-cob(I)amide&lt;/a&gt; cofactor. It is membrane-associated, with a C-terminal transmembrane helix [&lt;a href=&quot;http://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pubmed/8898900&quot;&gt;Harms96a&lt;/a&gt;, &lt;a href=&quot;http://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pubmed/9461302&quot;&gt;Harms97&lt;/a&gt;].
&lt;p&gt;
MtrA is not homologous to any known cobamide-binding proteins and is proposed as the motor of the sodium-ion pump. 
Genome analysis has revealed an extra copy or copies of the mtrA gene (named mtrA2) in many methanogenic archaea (e.g., &lt;a href=&quot;/META/NEW-IMAGE?object=TAX-145263&quot;&gt;&lt;i&gt;Methanothermobacter marburgensis&lt;/i&gt;&lt;/a&gt;, &lt;a href=&quot;/META/NEW-IMAGE?object=TAX-2162&quot;&gt;&lt;i&gt;Methanobacterium formicicum&lt;/i&gt;&lt;/a&gt;, &lt;a href=&quot;/META/NEW-IMAGE?object=TAX-83816&quot;&gt;&lt;i&gt;Methanobrevibacter ruminantium&lt;/i&gt;&lt;/a&gt;, &lt;a href=&quot;/META/NEW-IMAGE?object=TAX-2180&quot;&gt;&lt;i&gt;Methanothermus fervidus&lt;/i&gt;&lt;/a&gt; and &lt;a href=&quot;/META/NEW-IMAGE?object=TAX-2208&quot;&gt;&lt;i&gt;Methanosarcina barkeri&lt;/i&gt;&lt;/a&gt;). These MtrA homologues encode cytoplasmic proteins that consist only of a soluble cobalamin-binding domain of 170-180 amino acids, which is in contrast to the approximately 250 amino acids of membrane-associated MtrA. MtrA homologues have also been identified in the genomes of some bacteria and non-methanogenic archaea.
&lt;p&gt;
Crystal structures are available for the soluble domain of MtrA from &lt;a href=&quot;/META/NEW-IMAGE?object=TAX-2190&quot;&gt;&lt;i&gt;Methanocaldococcus jannaschii&lt;/i&gt;&lt;/a&gt; and the cytoplasmic MtrA homologue/cobalamin complex from &lt;a href=&quot;/META/NEW-IMAGE?object=TAX-2180&quot;&gt;&lt;i&gt;Methanothermus fervidus&lt;/i&gt;&lt;/a&gt;. The MtrA fold was found to correspond to the Rossmann-type &amp;alpha;/&amp;beta; fold, which is also found in many cobalamin-containing proteins. However, the cobamide-binding site of MtrA differ greatly from all the other cobalamin-binding sites [&lt;a href=&quot;http://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pubmed/27324530&quot;&gt;Wagner16&lt;/a&gt;].</comment>
